EST_LOCAL`, `PROVIDER_REMOTE`, `PROVIDER_OVERHEAD`, `SHARED`, `UNATTRIBUTED`.

## PM-RESOURCE-003 — No Silent Remote/Guest Aggregation

Remote/guest/Provider values never silently enter Host-local totals.

## PM-RESOURCE-004 — Anti-Double-Counting

Explicit rules prevent naive double counting of tmpfs, zram, integrated-GPU shared memory, guest memory plus Host VM process, reflink/shared storage, and similar shared resources.

## PM-VALUE-001 — Common Observation Value States

Where applicable distinguish `PRESENT`, `UNKNOWN`, `UNAVAILABLE`, `UNSUPPORTED`, `NOT_APPLICABLE`, `REDACTED`, `TRUNCATED`. Disclosure may omit field/object entirely if existence is protected.

## PM-LIMIT-001 — Limit States

Distinguish `VALUE`, `ZERO`, `UNSET`, `INHERITED`, `DEFAULT_AUTO`, `UNLIMITED`, `DISABLED`, `UNSUPPORTED`, `UNAVAILABLE`, `UNKNOWN`.

## PM-RESOURCE-005 — Resource Control Families

Separate scheduler priority, CPU weight, CPU quota, affinity/cpuset, memory, swap, process count, runtime duration, I/O weight/bandwidth, storage quota, GPU, network, and device controls.

## PM-RESOURCE-006 — Enforcement Layer

Every control names the layer that actually enforces it, such as Host cgroup, VM allocation, guest workload, container, or remote Provider quota.

## PM-RESOURCE-007 — Declared vs Effective

Keep declared Policy, effective enforcement, inherited constraints, runtime override, external/Provider constraints, emergency clamps, and drift separate.

## PM-RESOURCE-008 — Enforcement Strength

Examples include `HARD`, `PROVIDER_ENFORCED`, `BEST_EFFORT`, `ADVISORY`, `UNKNOWN`.

## PM-RESOURCE-009 — Enforcement Readback

Requested setting is not proof. Read effective state where mechanically possible.

## PM-CPU-001 — Scalable CPU Affinity

No fixed 32/64-bit mask. CPU sets/ranges bind topology revision and execution layer, including guest-vCPU vs Host-CPU scope.

## PM-CPU-002 — Scheduler Priority

Prime exposes bounded canonical priority intent plus backend-native/effective detail. Unsafe real-time classes require separate authority/proof.

## PM-OVERRIDE-001 — Runtime Override Lifecycle

Override records identity, source, revision, scope, direction, expiry, revocation, Core-restart behavior, reboot behavior, and effective readback.

## PM-OVERRIDE-002 — Override Direction Classes

Each mutable field declares tighten-only, bounded ordinary change, elevated broadening, or future-launch-only semantics.

## PM-OVERRIDE-003 — Broadening Expiry Guarantee

Temporary broadening is offered only where the authoritative enforcement layer can guarantee or reconcile expiry/revocation after client/Core failure.

## PM-POLREV-001 — Persistent Profile/Policy Revision

Persistent changes use immutable base + coherent derived revision + CAS + atomic public commit. Concurrent writers conflict. Revert creates/selects a new revision rather than rewriting history.

## PM-POLREV-002 — Running Workload Pinning

Running workload remains pinned to the admitted revision unless a separate runtime-override/control effect changes current effective state.

## PM-POLREV-003 — Component Update / Override Rebase

When a signed component definition changes, Prime explicitly revalidates/rebases Host overrides or reports conflict; it never silently discards or reinterprets them.

## PM-HW-001 — Live HardwareGraph / Capability Projection

PM1 uses revisioned live Host hardware, capability, and Provider projections. Startup-only snapshots are insufficient. Attach/remove/activation/health changes update without restarting Manager.

## PM-HW-002 — Host Hardware Scope

Top-level Hardware/Storage/Network/Thermal surfaces represent the current Prime Host. Guest/remote hardware appears only in an explicit nested target/execution context.

## PM-HW-003 — Device Identity Strength

Device/interface/storage/sensor identity declares strength/scope such as persistent, serial-backed, topology-scoped, boot-scoped, session-scoped, ambiguous.

## PM-HW-004 — Presence vs Assignment

Separate physically present, driver bound, Host available, passed through, assigned to VM/Provider, and unavailable.

