# W. QUIESCENCE, TRANSITIONS, DOMAIN TRANSACTIONS, AND ARTIFACT FOUNDATION

## PM-QUIESCE-001 — QuiescenceAssessment

Includes transition kind, blockers, closure/coverage, authority/epoch, relevant revisions, Impact/Protection references, expiry, and fence/token.

## PM-QUIESCE-002 — Quiescence Results

Portable results include `SAFE`, `BLOCKED`, `REQUIRES_CONFIRMATION`, `UNKNOWN`. SAFE requires sufficient authoritative coverage, not merely “all visible workloads looked fine.”

## PM-QUIESCE-003 — Transition-Specific Scope

No generic safe/quiescence badge. Assessment is explicitly scoped to REBOOT, SUSPEND, GENERATION_SWITCH, etc.

## PM-QUIESCE-004 — Transition Commit Fence

Immediately before destructive handoff, transition authority revalidates, enters quiescing, blocks/classifies newly arriving conflicting workloads/actions/transactions/diagnostics, then commits.

## PM-TRANS-001 — Transition Lease

Contains owner transaction, transition authority epoch, incompatible-transition scope, fence, acquisition, renewal, loss, reconciliation, and release semantics.

## PM-TRANS-002 — Transition Mutual Exclusion

Incompatible transitions such as reboot/rollback/Recovery boot cannot commit concurrently.

## PM-TRANS-003 — Transition Lease Loss

Timeout/owner death after quiescing/destructive progress does not automatically free the Host for another transition; the prior transaction must be reconciled/fenced first.

## PM-TRANS-004 — Transition Abort

If preparation changes Host state and transition fails, record actual preparatory effects, run defined compensations where safe, and allow PARTIAL/REQUIRES_OPERATOR rather than fabricating restoration.

## PM-HANDOFF-001 — Recovery Handoff Marker

Small, generation-independent, Host/lineage-bound, versioned, tamper-evident, secret-minimal, early-Recovery-readable, one-transition correlation record. It is evidence/handoff state, not a reusable command.

## PM-HANDOFF-002 — Handoff Replay/Compatibility

After reconciliation the marker becomes consumed/historical. Older Recovery that cannot authenticate/understand a newer marker preserves/ignores safely and does not claim transition correlation.

## PM-DTX-001 — Domain Transaction Model

Domain-owned long lifecycle such as update, rollback, component transaction, Recovery, or system transition.

## PM-DTX-002 — Transaction Fields

Stable transaction identity, domain owner/authority epoch, lifecycle/phase, outcome, progress semantics, blockers, continuity/durability class, cancellation availability, request-idempotency identity, limitations, disclosure projection.

## PM-DTX-003 — Current-State/Event Observation

Current transaction state appears in Current-State Projection; transitions may appear in Mechanical Events. Domain authority owns durable truth.

## PM-DTX-004 — Durable Creation / Lost Response

Same request ID + same canonical digest reconciles same created object; same ID + different digest conflicts. Creation record is durable before irreversible work. Authorized recent-object discovery exists if client loses returned ID.

## PM-DTX-005 — Rollback Compatibility

Older authority preserves unknown newer transaction state and reports unsupported/unresolved rather than deleting or inventing completion.

## PM-DTX-006 — System Transition Specialization

Reboot/shutdown/suspend/generation-switch/rollback/Recovery mutation uses Domain Transaction semantics when that capability is implemented. PM1 may initially observe transition state without exposing every transition action.

## PM-ARTIFACT-001 — Artifact Security Foundation

Foundational invariants: effective classification floor before byte 1, immutable canonical artifact identity/body, digest/version, generation separate from release authorization, partial output remains classified, strong disposal not claimed from ordinary unlink on COW storage.

## PM-ARTIFACT-002 — Concrete Artifact Jobs Are Phase-Scoped

Concrete long-running export/diagnostic ArtifactJob lifecycle enters the phase that first implements the feature through the Contract Delta Gate; it is not an artificial PM1 prerequisite.

