# X. TELEMETRY, EVENTS, SUBSCRIPTIONS, AND CONTROL-PLANE ISOLATION

## PM-TELEM-001 — Telemetry Semantics

Telemetry is sampled, bounded, potentially lossy, explicit about gaps, and separate from lifecycle truth.

## PM-TELEM-002 — Counter Semantics

Counters declare width, monotonicity, reset epoch/token, wrap behavior, source, and scope.

## PM-TELEM-003 — Percentage Denominator

Every percentage identifies its denominator/basis.

## PM-TELEM-004 — Source/Schema Replacement

Existing streams pin source/Provider/schema semantics. Replacement continues only under explicit compatible semantics; otherwise reset/re-negotiate.

## PM-TELEM-005 — Historical Telemetry

History preserves time range, resolution, aggregation, source epochs, gaps, resets, denominator changes, and does not silently smooth semantic discontinuities.

## PM-TIMEOBS-001 — Uptime/Duration Reset Domains

Every displayed uptime/duration names the reset domain—Host boot, Core instance, Provider session, workload instance, service activation—and whether suspend time counts.

## PM-EVENT-001 — Mechanical Event Contract

Events define identity, delivery semantics, replay horizon, deduplication, overflow, authorized-data gaps, source/epoch, and version behavior.

## PM-EVENT-002 — Source Loss Is Not Object Death

Provider/source loss does not synthesize process/service/workload exit.

## PM-EVENT-003 — Unknown Event Semantics

Older client may ignore only event classes explicitly declared additive/safe-to-ignore, and then history interpretation is marked PARTIAL. Unknown safety-significant semantics force reset/renegotiation rather than permissive drop.

## PM-SUB-001 — Subscription Lease/Liveness

Projection/telemetry/event subscriptions have owner-bound leases, heartbeat/liveness semantics, source state, expiry, and cleanup after client crash.

## PM-SUB-002 — Shared Observation Collectors

Compatible collectors/Providers may be shared/reference-counted. Closing one Manager instance releases only its lease; it does not stop a collector still required by other clients/system authorities.

## PM-CTRLPLANE-001 — Traffic-Class Isolation

Security/recovery/control traffic has reserved queue/service capacity isolated from telemetry/diagnostics via separate bounded channels/queues or guaranteed priority multiplexing.

## PM-CTRLPLANE-002 — Client/Source Fairness

Within traffic classes, Core enforces per-ManagementControlSession/client/system/principal/source and global limits for concurrency, rate, outstanding preflights, subscriptions, diagnostics, and work budget. Many sessions cannot evade quotas.

